      "title": "Political Parties and Currents in Iran | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Understand Iran’s parties, reformist and principlist currents, electoral alliances, and the distinction between legal recognition and political influence."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "parties-currents-and-alliances",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Three overlapping forms of political organization",
        "content": "A party, a political current, and an electoral coalition describe different things in Iran. Parties have a legal framework and organizational rules. Currents are broader political families whose members may belong to different organizations. Coalitions coordinate support around an election or shared objective. Treating these terms as interchangeable obscures how political competition works. [s1][s2][s3]\n\nThe distinction also explains an apparent contradiction in English-language accounts. Some describe Iran as lacking political parties, while others discuss registered organizations. ACCORD’s review documents both descriptions: the first generally concerns the weakness of disciplined, programmatic party competition; the second concerns legal registration. Neither terminology should substitute for examining an organization’s actual activities. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "understanding-political-labels",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "What the major labels explain",
        "content": "Reformists and principlists are useful starting points, but they are not cohesive parties. Principlist generally refers to political actors emphasizing the Islamic Republic’s revolutionary foundations and conservative political and cultural positions. Reformist has commonly described efforts to expand political participation and civil freedoms through change within the existing system. These descriptions concern tendencies rather than a uniform position held by every politician. [s3][s4]\n\nOther analytical categories cut across that division. In a study based partly on interviews with Iranian officials and analysts, the European Council on Foreign Relations distinguished modernisers, principlists, and security-linked actors it called securocrats. Its moderniser category brought together reformist and pragmatic figures interested in international engagement. This was an analytical framework developed for a particular period, rather than an official classification or permanent map of Iranian society. [s3]\n\nEconomic policy, international diplomacy, cultural restrictions, and access to state institutions do not necessarily divide politicians along identical lines. A broad factional label therefore needs a date, a policy context, and an explanation of the alliance being discussed. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s3", "s4"]
      },
      {
        "id": "competition-and-institutional-limits",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Competition within a restricted political field",
        "content": "Political organization takes place within constitutional limits. Article 26 permits parties and associations subject to conditions concerning national independence, unity, Islamic criteria, and the foundations of the Islamic Republic. This formal provision does not by itself establish how freely a particular organization can operate. [s5]\n\nCandidate eligibility is another distinct constraint. International Crisis Group’s research on the March 2024 parliamentary election documented exclusions and disagreements among reformists over whether participation remained worthwhile. Its assessment was that candidate numbers concealed a narrowly bounded contest. That interpretation concerns the quality of competition, rather than simply the presence or absence of registered organizations. [s6]\n\nFormal affiliation is also an incomplete guide to practical influence. ECFR describes factional competition extending through elected offices, unelected institutions, and security networks. Electoral success consequently needs to be considered alongside access to other centers of authority. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s3", "s5", "s6"]
      },
      {
        "id": "changing-alliances",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Why a dated account matters",
        "content": "The 2016 List of Hope illustrates the breadth an electoral alliance can acquire. Carnegie’s analysis describes a heterogeneous grouping of reformists, centrists, and some traditional conservatives. Sharing an electoral list did not erase their disagreements. [s7]\n\nEvents in February 2026 underline another distinction: participation in recognized political networks does not establish protection from arrest. Reuters reported the release on bail of Reform Front figures Azar Mansouri, Javad Emam, and Ebrahim Asgharzadeh, citing their lawyer through the ISNA news agency. The lawyer said he had not been told what charges they might face. That report establishes a dated development, not their subsequent legal position. [s8]\n\nA useful reading of Iranian politics therefore follows organizations, endorsements, institutional access, and documented restrictions together. It also leaves room for people whose views cannot be inferred from the labels used by competing political elites. [s2][s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s2", "s3", "s7", "s8"]
      }
    ],
