    "seo": {
      "title": "Registered Political Parties in Iran | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Explore Iran’s legal framework for political parties, organizational requirements, operating licenses, and the limits of available registration evidence."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "constitutional-foundation",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Recognition within a conditional framework",
        "content": "The constitutional starting point is Article 26. It permits political parties and several other forms of association, while making that permission conditional on principles including national unity, Islamic criteria, and the foundations of the Islamic Republic. It also prohibits compelling people to participate in those associations or preventing participation in them. These provisions establish the constitutional language; they do not prove that every political tendency can obtain recognition or operate without interference. [s1]\n\nThe International Labour Organization’s NATLEX database records a Law on Activities of Political Parties and Groups with a text date of 16 November 2016. Its summary identifies chapters on definitions, establishment, and rights and responsibilities, and notes ideological requirements and exclusions from membership. The database entry is a useful identification aid, but the underlying Persian legislation is the stronger source for specific provisions. [s2][s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "organizational-form",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "What the legislation calls a party",
        "content": "Article 1 describes a party as an organization pursuing a defined political program under an approved charter and manifesto, operating nationally or provincially within the Islamic Republic’s laws. It recognizes that such an organization may use titles other than the word party. Translating a name as an association, society, or organization therefore does not settle its legal classification. [s3]\n\nThe law identifies organizational roles including a general assembly, central council, secretary-general, inspectors, and provincial branch officials. It distinguishes an initial establishment permit from a final operating license. Those distinctions matter when reading announcements: permission to begin establishing an organization should not automatically be reported as a completed operating license. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "registration-and-political-weight",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A register is not a measure of influence",
        "content": "Registration counts answer an administrative question, not a political one. ACCORD’s 2017 review juxtaposed reports of numerous registered parties with research describing candidate-centered elections and weak party discipline. Its sources emphasized the importance of personalities and shifting factions. This helps explain why a legally recognized organization may be less visible than an electoral alliance or influential individual. [s4]\n\nThose older observations should not become a blanket assertion that Iranian parties have no offices, members, or programs. The legislation itself provides an organizational framework. The useful question is which activities can be demonstrated for a particular party at a particular date. [s3][s4]",
        "source_ids": ["s3", "s4"]
      },
      {
        "id": "directory-evidence",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "What remains necessary for a reliable directory",
        "content": "A publication-ready directory requires a dated register and evidence for each entry’s license, exact name, geographical scope, and subsequent administrative changes. Historical mentions cannot establish that an organization remained licensed at the research cutoff. This page therefore explains the framework without presenting an unverified current roster.\n\nReaders should also distinguish an organization’s legal state from the position of its officers. Reuters’ February 2026 reporting on reformist figures released on bail concerns individuals and a coalition; it does not establish the cancellation or continuation of any constituent party’s license. [s5]",
        "source_ids": ["s5"]
      }
    ],
