    "description": "Iranian political coalitions coordinate parties, politicians, and supporters around shared electoral or political objectives. Their composition and influence vary between elections, and a common endorsement does not necessarily establish shared party membership or lasting parliamentary discipline.",
    "tags": ["Political coalitions", "Electoral alliances", "Candidate lists", "List of Hope", "Reform Front"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-27T23:59:36Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-27T23:59:36Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Political Coalitions and Electoral Alliances in Iran | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Examine Iranian electoral alliances through the List of Hope and the 2024 elections, with distinctions between endorsements, parties, and political currents."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "coalition-meaning",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A shared objective without a single organization",
        "content": "A coalition allows political actors to cooperate without becoming one party. In Iran, a legal definition and a broader electoral usage coexist. Article 1 of the party law defines a front as a political coalition of licensed parties formed for a specified activity with notification to the parties commission. An alliance described in election reporting should not automatically be assumed to satisfy that legal definition. [s1]\n\nThe political task is often to coordinate support around candidates who can compete. Such coordination can bring together actors with different priorities. An endorsement establishes support in a particular contest; it does not establish that a candidate belongs to every endorsing organization. Carnegie’s account of the 2016 election provides a clear example of that distinction. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "hope-list",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "The List of Hope as a historical example",
        "content": "The List of Hope brought reformists and centrists together with some traditional conservatives in the 2016 parliamentary election. Former president Mohammad Khatami encouraged voters to support the list. Carnegie’s analysis emphasizes its heterogeneity, including the presence of socially conservative politician Ali Motahari. [s2]\n\nThe lesson is more specific than a simple reformist-versus-conservative result. Cooperation could be organized around opposition to harder-line rivals and support for aspects of President Hassan Rouhani’s agenda despite disagreements on other questions. The coalition’s breadth was a source of electoral opportunity, but its members’ different positions also made a single ideological description inadequate. This is a dated case, not evidence that the same alliance persisted unchanged. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "fragmentation-in-2024",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Coordination and fragmentation in 2024",
        "content": "The March 2024 parliamentary contest exposed divisions within both broad political camps. International Crisis Group identified competing conservative campaigns associated with the Coalition Council, the Unity Council, and harder-line groups, particularly the Endurance Front. Those divisions show why principlist should not be read as the name of a single electoral organization. [s3]\n\nReformist participation was also divided. Crisis Group distinguished the Reformist Front’s refusal to endorse candidates from the participation of other reformist and centrist actors. The position of one umbrella body therefore did not establish the conduct of every politician described as reformist. [s3]\n\nThe presidential election later that year produced a different alignment. Crisis Group’s interview-based report describes reformist and centrist support for Masoud Pezeshkian, while conservative rivals Mohammad Baqer Qalibaf and Saeed Jalili competed rather than agreeing on an early withdrawal. The eventual Pezeshkian victory demonstrated that the parliamentary contest had not fixed every subsequent electoral relationship. [s4]",
        "source_ids": ["s3", "s4"]
      },
      {
        "id": "reading-coalition-claims",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "How to interpret a coalition claim",
        "content": "Three questions make coalition reporting clearer: which contest is involved, who issued the endorsement, and what evidence establishes membership or cooperation? A campaign alliance should also be distinguished from the working relationships that emerge after an election. Crisis Group’s discussion of Pezeshkian’s transition describes efforts to solicit cabinet suggestions across factional lines, illustrating a separate form of cooperation. [s4]\n\nCoalitions also face constraints beyond internal disagreement. Reuters reported the February 2026 release on bail of the Reform Front’s head and other prominent figures. The report did not establish their later legal position or the coalition’s operating capacity at the October cutoff. A current directory of alliances consequently requires fresh organizational and legal evidence. [s5]",
        "source_ids": ["s4", "s5"]
      }
    ],
